        "title": "Comparing Text and Performance",
        "content": `The application for this final lesson is a comparative method you can use with any adapted play: read a scene in Williams's text, then watch or study how a specific production stages it, and treat every difference as evidence about interpretation and about the pressures acting on the adaptation. Take the ending. First, establish exactly what Williams wrote: Blanche is led away by the Doctor after her line about the kindness of strangers; Stella sobs "with inhuman abandon" but lets her sister go; Stanley kneels beside Stella and, in the play's final image, his fingers find "the opening of her blouse," while Steve deals the next hand of poker and the blue piano and the "muted trumpet" swell. Life resumes. Now compare the 1951 film's ending, with Stella's flight up the stairs, and ask a precise question: what does each ending claim about the world? Williams's claim is that desire and ordinary life simply close over the tragedy; the film's claim, forced by censors, is that virtue is restored and the guilty punished. Neither is an accident; each encodes a worldview, one tragic and one consoling. Then widen the method. Compare Tandy's and Leigh's arrivals in Scene One and ask how sane each Blanche appears and what that does to your sympathy. Compare a modern stage revival, which can show everything the 1951 film had to hide, and ask what is gained and what, surprisingly, is lost when the shadow-and-symbol restraint of the censored film gives way to explicitness. The discipline throughout is to treat performance choices as readings, arguments about what the play means, made in the concrete language of casting, staging, cutting, and framing. A student who can move fluently between Williams's printed text and its major performances, naming what each production amplifies and suppresses and why, understands Streetcar far more deeply than one who has only read the words on the page, because this is a play that has always lived, and changed, in the act of being performed.`,
